Answer a zero-length body carrying Expect: 100-continue instead of refusing it.

Drogon's request parser rejects any HTTP/1.1 request that carries
`Expect: 100-continue` together with `Content-Length: 0`, returning 400 before
the request reaches a handler. Every S3 client writes a zero-byte object
exactly that way, so without this patch `boto3.put_object(Body=b"")`,
`aws s3 cp` of an empty file, and therefore `aws s3 sync` of any tree
containing one, all fail with an unexplained 400.

RFC 7231 §5.1.1 makes 100-continue an optimisation for withholding a body the
server may not want. With no body to withhold there is nothing to negotiate,
and the request is already complete: the only correct action is to handle it.

Present in v1.9.9 and still on upstream master as of 2026-08. Reported
upstream; drop this patch once a release carries the fix. Should the file move
or the surrounding lines change, `git apply` fails and the configure step stops
— which is the intended behaviour, not a nuisance: it forces a look at whether
the bug is still there.

diff --git a/lib/src/HttpRequestParser.cc b/lib/src/HttpRequestParser.cc
index a42a83f..aa7a29b 100644
--- a/lib/src/HttpRequestParser.cc
+++ b/lib/src/HttpRequestParser.cc
@@ -278,8 +278,12 @@ int HttpRequestParser::parseRequest(MsgBuffer *buf)
                 {
                     if (remainContentLength_ == 0)
                     {
-                        // error
-                        return -k400BadRequest;
+                        // There is no body to wait for, so there is nothing to
+                        // withhold: the request is already complete. Fall
+                        // through and let it be handled. Rejecting it breaks
+                        // every S3 client that writes a zero-byte object, all
+                        // of which send Expect: 100-continue with
+                        // Content-Length: 0.
                     }
                     else
                     {
